      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "🎧 Palestra (professor lê sem pausas, ≈3 min):"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "blockquote",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "“Good morning. Today we'll trace the history of glass, from ancient times to the present. Let's begin with its origins. The earliest glass objects we know of are small beads, made in Mesopotamia roughly four and a half thousand years ago. Nobody knows exactly how glass was discovered, but one theory is that it happened by accident, when sand was heated near a fire used for cooking or metalwork."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Moving on to the Roman period. The great breakthrough was the invention of glassblowing, which allowed glass to be shaped quickly. As a result, glass became cheaper and more common — ordinary families could own glass cups. The Romans even used glass in windows, although it wasn't very clear; you could see light, but not much detail."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Now let's turn to medieval Europe, and in particular Venice. In 1291, the city ordered its glassmakers to move to the island of Murano. The official reason was the risk of fire in a city of wooden buildings — though it also helped keep their techniques secret. In fact, glassmakers were not permitted to leave the island without permission."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Finally, modern glass. In the 1950s, a British company developed the float process, in which molten glass is poured onto a bed of liquid tin. This produced perfectly flat glass, ideal for buildings and cars. Today, researchers are working on glass that can repair itself when it's scratched. And what's particularly interesting from an environmental point of view is that glass can be recycled again and again without losing quality. The main challenge is practical: collecting it and separating the different colours, which can't be mixed."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "To sum up, a material discovered by accident has become one of the most important in modern life.”"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
